<BaseLayout title="When is automation worth building? | Wear Valley Digital" description="A practical way to assess repeated work, existing tools, exceptions and the running costs of business automation." canonical="https://wearvalleydigital.com/insights/when-to-automate/" disableAnalytics={true}>
  <section class="hero"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">Buyer guide</p><h1>When is automation worth building?</h1><p class="lede">Start with the repeated work and the result you need. Then decide whether software will help.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section"><article class="wrap prose">
    <p>By Wear Valley Digital · Updated <time datetime="2026-10-02">2 October 2026</time></p>
    <h2>Describe the work as it happens today</h2>
    <p>Write down what starts the task, what information it uses, who handles it and how often it happens. Include the time spent checking results and correcting mistakes. Identify the useful outcome, rather than starting with a particular tool.</p>
    <p>For example, copying enquiry details between systems may be a repeated task. The outcome is a reliable record that reaches the right person, including enquiries that need manual attention.</p>
    <h2>Check what you already have</h2>
    <p>Look for an existing feature, configuration or connection that meets the need. A small change to an established system may be enough. Reuse should reduce repeated work while preserving the permissions and data boundaries of each business.</p>
    <p>Confirm access, usage charges and licence conditions before depending on a service. A connection shown in a demonstration does not establish that it can run in your account.</p>
    <h2>Include exceptions in the scope</h2>
    <p>Describe missing information, duplicate requests, failed connections and cases that need a person to decide. Agree what the software may do automatically, what needs approval and how a person can correct or recover a failed task.</p>
    <p>A repeated task with clear rules is easier to evaluate than a process whose decisions are still changing. Where judgement matters, keeping a person in the workflow may be part of the design.</p>
    <h2>Compare the whole cost</h2>
    <p>Compare the current work with the cost of building, operating and maintaining the proposed solution. Include service usage, checking, support and future changes. Use your actual task volumes and observations; a demo cannot establish savings for your business.</p>
    <h2>Try one bounded workflow</h2>
    <p>Choose a small example with an agreed success check. Test it with suitable sample data and check that retries do not duplicate the result. Confirm that it respects account permissions and that failures are visible before using it for real work.</p>
    <p>Use what you learn to decide whether to extend the workflow, change it or keep the existing approach.</p>
